// name Editions
// id editions
// +frag.id.show -
// brief The editions of 1939, 1958 and 2010, the witnesses of the text, and the apparatus.
// C A control card: spaced, justified paragraphs; the headwords of list items in bold.
// +html.style p { margin: 0.6em 0; text-align: justify; }
// +bd ^(– )?(?P<open>)(?:(?!\. )[^:]){1,40}?(?P<close>)(?=: )
// +bd ^(?P<open>)(\d{4}|[A-Z])(?P<close>)(?=\. )
The text of this deck is established from four witnesses: two scanned
printings and two independent transcriptions. The apparatus records the
differences between three editions: the two scanned printings, the first
edition of 1939 and the corrected printing of 1958, and the edition of 2010
by Danis Rose and John O'Hanlon, which revises the text throughout.
/ id top

; head
/ level 3
/ id printings
The editions

> 1939. James Joyce, _Finnegans Wake_. London: Faber and Faber; New York: The
  Viking Press, 4 May 1939. Set in Monotype Fournier and printed by R.
  MacLehose and Company, The University Press, Glasgow. The first edition: the
  British and American issues were printed from the same setting. The copy
  used is the scan of the Faber issue in the Digital Library of India (from
  the Rashtrapati Bhavan Library).
/ id 1939
/ += . "the scan of the Faber issue" -> https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.207614

> 1958. James Joyce, _Finnegans Wake_. New York: The Viking Press, 1958 and
  later printings. Photo-offset from the plates of the first edition, with
  Joyce's "Corrections of Misprints" (printed separately in 1945 and bound
  into copies from 1947) entered into the text. Some corrections required
  resetting a passage, so that a few lines are divided differently from 1939.
  The copy used is the scan from the University of Toronto (Fisher Library).
/ id 1958
/ += . "the scan from the University of Toronto" -> https://archive.org/details/finneganswake00joycuoft

> 2010. James Joyce, _Finnegans Wake_, edited by Danis Rose and John
  O'Hanlon. Mousehole: Houyhnhnm Press, 2010; reprinted as _The Restored
  Finnegans Wake_, London: Penguin Classics, 2012. Some 9 000 changes to the
  text of 1939, of words, punctuation and spacing, and passages restored, from
  the manuscripts, typescripts and proofs. Set anew, in 493 pages: its pages
  and lines are not those of 1939. Not available as images; its text, with
  every change marked, is on the James Joyce Digital Archive.
/ id 2010
/ += . "James Joyce\s+Digital\s+Archive" -> https://jjda.ie/f/fwhome.htm

The page and line division of 1939 and 1958 is the same (except where noted
in the apparatus), and is that of all the later Faber and Viking printings
and of the line numbers used by Fweet. The pages and lines here are those of
1939 and 1958; the edition of 2010 enters only through the apparatus, and at
the foot of each page, through a link to the pages of 2010 that it spans.
/ += . "line numbers used by Fweet" -> https://fweet.org/pages/fw_lnum.php

; head
/ level 3
/ id transcriptions
The transcriptions

> W. The transcription by David Wilson of the Faber edition (1964), preserving
  the line and page breaks. Oxford Text Archive 1709 (1992; archived copy).
/ id W
/ += . "Oxford Text Archive 1709" -> https://ota.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/repository/xmlui/handle/20.500.12024/1709
/ += . "archived copy" -> https://web.archive.org/web/20250913120344/https://ota.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/repository/xmlui/handle/20.500.12024/1709

> T. The transcription by Donald F. Theall, Joan Benedict Theall and Ray
  Fritz-Németh of the Faber second edition (1950), with the published errata
  incorporated; the "Trent text", the source of most electronic texts of
  the book. Oxford Text Archive 1696 (1992; archived copy).
/ id T
/ += . "Oxford Text Archive 1696" -> https://ota.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/repository/xmlui/handle/20.500.12024/1696
/ += . "archived copy" -> https://web.archive.org/web/20260305211945/https://ota.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/repository/xmlui/handle/20.500.12024/1696

Both are distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0
licence. Each has its own errors (Fweet lists more than three hundred in T):
lost and doubled lines, dropped words, misreadings.
/ += . "Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0" -> https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/
/ += . "Fweet lists" -> https://fweet.org/pages/fw_typo.php

; head
/ level 3
/ id text
The established text

The text is that of the corrected Faber and Viking printings. Where W and T
agree, their reading is taken; where they disagree, the reading of the
printings, as read from the scans, decides. A little under three hundred
places were decided by looking at the page images. See Tools.
/ += . "Tools" -> !tools

Joyce's sigla, the signs for the characters of the book (*E* for
Humphrey Chimpden Earwicker, *A* for Anna Livia Plurabelle and so on), are
set in the Fweet Figla font of Raphael Slepon, after a font of Michael
Powers, with thanks. In the deck files a siglum is written as Fweet writes
it, the letter between asterisks. The few sigla that the font lacks (the
turned and reversed forms on 18.36, 36.17, 121.03, 121.07 and 266.22) are
written with the closest Unicode characters. The musical scores and the
figures are images cropped from the 1939 scan.
/ id sigla
/ += . "Fweet Figla font" -> https://www.fweet.org/pages/fw_font.php

; head
/ level 3
/ id notes
The notes

The right margin carries the notes "ט word" (tet), which link the word to its
gloss on finwake.com; they are made by aligning the text of finwake.com, where the
glossed words are links, with the text of this deck (about 51 000 glosses,
some on parts of words; about 500 could not be placed). Only the links are
here, not the glosses. Where finwake.com links a gloss to an anchor that its
page does not have, the link goes to the anchor that was meant; about 2 400
glosses that are not on the free pages of finwake.com are left out.
/ += . "finwake\.com" -> https://finwake.com/

The notes "Ω phrase: entry" (omega) link a phrase to the
entry of the notebook (or notesheet, or copybook) in which Joyce noted it, as
transcribed on the James Joyce Digital Archive, where these links are called
"notons". The entry is named as on the archive: the notebook, its page and
the item on the page ("N18 (VI.B.8): 185(i)"). The archive links about
24 000 phrases of the text to their entries; about 23 600 are given here.
The link goes to the phrase in the archive's text of 1939: clicking the
phrase there shows the entry in a window, with its source, its use in the
drafts and links to them. This works for every notebook, although most
full transcriptions of the notebooks are not public on the archive (the
Joyce estate did not permit their publication). Only the links are taken
from the archive: no part of its transcriptions is copied here.
/ += . "James Joyce\s+Digital\s+Archive" -> https://jjda.ie/f/fwhome.htm

Two kinds of notes are footnotes, at the foot of the page, each starting with
the number of its line, and underlined in the same colour as the words of the
line it refers to: the notes of the apparatus (below), which start with ב,
and the notes "ק" (qof, for "qol", voice), which mark the lines where
recorded readings start and link to the recording at that point; the places
are found by matching the automatic captions of the recordings against the
text.

; head
/ level 3
/ id apparatus
The apparatus

Where 1939, 1958 and the edition of 2010 differ, a footnote on the line gives
the variant, in the usual form of a critical apparatus, at the foot of the
page as in a printed edition. To tell them from other notes, they start with the letter beth, ב
(for "variorum"):
/ id form

; code
|ב lemma] SIGLUM; reading SIGLUM

The lemma is the reading of the established text, followed by the sigla of
the editions that have it; after the semicolon are the other readings, each
followed by the sigla of the editions that have it. "om." means that the printing omits the lemma. For example, on
4.14 the established text reads "was", as does 1958, while 1939 reads "waz":
/ += . "4\.14" -> !p004-14

; code
|ב was] 1958; waz 1939

and on 3.02 the established text, with 1939 and 1958, reads "commodius",
where 2010 reads "commodious":
/ += . "3\.02" -> !p003-02

; code
|ב commodius] 1939 1958; commodious 2010

A passage that the two printings divide into lines differently is marked on
its first line. Differences in spacing, broken or battered type and other
accidents of printing that are not readings are not recorded.

The differences were found by comparing the images of every word of the two
scans (as located by their OCR) and reading again, with a second OCR engine,
every word whose two images or two OCR readings differed; the remaining
candidates were then checked by eye. Differences that both OCR engines read
identically in both scans (for example, a changed punctuation mark that the
engines did not notice) can have been missed.

At the bottom of each page are links to the images of that page in both
printings, and to the corresponding pages of the edition of 2010.

; head
/ level 3
/ id rose
The variants of 2010

The variants of 2010 are those recorded by its editors on the James Joyce
Digital Archive, which shows, for every section of the book, the text of
1939 with every change of 2010 marked (the pages "1939 → 2010"); about 7 900
notes of the apparatus record them. Each change is placed on its line here
by aligning that text with the text of this deck. Where the established text
already agrees with 2010 (most often in Joyce's own corrections of 1939, which
2010 also adopts), there is no note of 2010, but the siglum 2010 is added to
the reading it shares in the note of 1939 and 1958. A reading of 2010 longer
than a dozen words is abbreviated, and linked to its place on the Archive.
About 650 changes (some in passages that 2010 moves, and in the marginalia
and footnotes of II.2) could not be placed. The edition of 2010 is in
copyright: its readings are cited here, as in any apparatus.

The text of 1939 on the Archive is not always that of the printing (it
corrects some misprints, and has a few errors of its own): it serves only to
place the changes. The readings of 1939 and 1958 in the notes are those of
the scans.

The foot of each page links to the pages of 2010 that correspond to it, on
the Archive, at the words with which the page begins (the link is a text
fragment: browsers that do not support them open the section at its top).
